1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,970
The swelling claim didn't die because a checklist document existed somewhere.

2
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:09,950
It died because Dana looked at it, said no, and everyone on the account had already

3
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:11,390
agreed her no was final.

4
00:00:11,390 --> 00:00:14,451
That's the part a checklist alone never guarantees.

5
00:00:14,451 --> 00:00:19,251
Everything from the last four lectures, claims, disclosure, rights, accuracy, is

6
00:00:19,251 --> 00:00:22,911
only real if someone is actually accountable for checking it.

7
00:00:22,911 --> 00:00:27,831
A checklist that exists in a document but belongs to nobody gets skipped the first

8
00:00:27,831 --> 00:00:31,791
time a deadline gets tight, which is exactly when it matters most.

9
00:00:31,791 --> 00:00:34,431
So this lecture answers two plain questions.

10
00:00:34,431 --> 00:00:35,271
Who signs off.

11
00:00:35,271 --> 00:00:40,192
And at what point in the process that sign-off actually happens, before generation

12
00:00:40,192 --> 00:00:41,992
starts, or right before spend.

13
00:00:41,992 --> 00:00:47,032
Why does this need its own lecture, rather than a line at the bottom of each earlier

14
00:00:47,032 --> 00:00:47,272
one?

15
00:00:47,272 --> 00:00:52,132
Because a check without an owner tends to quietly become everyone's job, which in

16
00:00:52,132 --> 00:00:54,112
practice means it's nobody's job.

17
00:00:54,112 --> 00:00:57,592
The four checks from this section are genuinely good ones.

18
00:00:57,592 --> 00:01:02,512
They still fail in the field constantly, and almost never because the check itself

19
00:01:02,512 --> 00:01:03,113
was wrong.

20
00:01:03,113 --> 00:01:07,133
They fail because nobody was actually on the hook for running them.

21
00:01:07,683 --> 00:01:12,620
Notice this table assigns a name to every row, not a vague "the team" or "whoever

22
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:17,557
has time." On a two-person account like Harlan's, that's Dana for claims, because

23
00:01:17,557 --> 00:01:22,677
she's the one who actually knows what the product can defend, and Sam for rights and

24
00:01:22,677 --> 00:01:27,797
disclosure, because he's the one touching the ad account where those checks actually

25
00:01:27,797 --> 00:01:28,102
bite.

26
00:01:28,102 --> 00:01:32,429
If you're running this solo, all four rows collapse to one name: yours.

27
00:01:32,429 --> 00:01:37,245
That's not a smaller job, it just means building the discipline to run all four

28
00:01:37,245 --> 00:01:42,304
checks yourself, deliberately, rather than assuming a second set of eyes will catch

29
00:01:42,304 --> 00:01:43,279
what you missed.

30
00:01:43,279 --> 00:01:47,911
Notice, too, that the table splits ownership by expertise, not by seniority.

31
00:01:47,911 --> 00:01:52,909
Dana owns claims because she's the one who actually knows the product, not because

32
00:01:52,909 --> 00:01:54,007
she's the founder.

33
00:01:54,007 --> 00:01:59,005
Sam owns rights because he's the one inside the ad account daily, not because it's

34
00:01:59,005 --> 00:02:00,224
beneath anyone else.

35
00:02:00,224 --> 00:02:05,283
Assign each check to whoever's closest to the actual knowledge it requires, and the

36
00:02:05,283 --> 00:02:08,513
check gets faster and more accurate as a side effect.

37
00:02:09,063 --> 00:02:13,898
Here's why this happens at three separate points instead of one big review at the

38
00:02:13,898 --> 00:02:14,137
end.

39
00:02:14,137 --> 00:02:18,017
Catching a claims problem before generation costs you a sentence.

40
00:02:18,017 --> 00:02:22,493
Catching the same problem after a batch is fully cut costs you a reshoot, a

41
00:02:22,493 --> 00:02:25,478
re-render, and the time already spent on assembly.

42
00:02:25,478 --> 00:02:30,492
The disclosure checkpoint sitting at upload, rather than earlier, is deliberate too.

43
00:02:30,492 --> 00:02:35,207
It's the one check that genuinely doesn't need to slow anything down before it,

44
00:02:35,207 --> 00:02:38,908
because adding a caption doesn't touch the script or the shot.

45
00:02:38,908 --> 00:02:43,921
Putting it last keeps the earlier checkpoints focused on the things that actually do

46
00:02:43,921 --> 00:02:45,831
require a redo if they're wrong.

47
00:02:45,831 --> 00:02:48,398
Three checkpoints isn't more work than one.

48
00:02:48,398 --> 00:02:53,412
It's the same total checking, moved earlier, where each individual catch is cheaper.

49
00:02:53,412 --> 00:02:58,187
That's the entire logic behind pre-flight QC: not doing more checking, doing the

50
00:02:58,187 --> 00:02:59,440
same checking sooner.

51
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:03,977
Ask yourself where your own current process actually catches problems today.

52
00:03:03,977 --> 00:03:08,394
If the honest answer is "whenever someone happens to notice," that's not a

53
00:03:08,394 --> 00:03:11,617
checkpoint, it's luck with a good track record so far.

54
00:03:11,617 --> 00:03:16,273
Luck runs out eventually, usually on the exact batch you were rushing to ship.

55
00:03:16,823 --> 00:03:19,693
One honest distinction worth being blunt about.

56
00:03:19,693 --> 00:03:24,579
A checklist written down somewhere is not the same thing as a checklist that can

57
00:03:24,579 --> 00:03:26,778
actually stop a batch from shipping.

58
00:03:26,778 --> 00:03:31,542
On Harlan's account, Dana's sign-off genuinely blocks a claim from going live,

59
00:03:31,542 --> 00:03:35,024
because everyone involved has agreed that's how it works.

60
00:03:35,024 --> 00:03:40,032
If your version of this is just a document nobody's actually empowered to enforce,

61
00:03:40,032 --> 00:03:41,742
it's decoration, not a gate.

62
00:03:41,742 --> 00:03:44,674
This matters especially once you're moving fast.

63
00:03:44,674 --> 00:03:49,743
Section nine's weekly sprint asks you to generate and ship at a real pace, and pace

64
00:03:49,743 --> 00:03:53,835
is exactly when an unenforced checklist gets quietly skipped first.

65
00:03:53,835 --> 00:03:58,782
Build the authority into the gate now, while there's no deadline pressure testing

66
00:03:58,782 --> 00:04:00,554
it, not later, once there is.

67
00:04:00,554 --> 00:04:04,096
Think about what "enforced" actually requires in practice.

68
00:04:04,096 --> 00:04:08,005
It doesn't need a legal department or a formal process document.

69
00:04:08,005 --> 00:04:13,074
It needs one simple rule everyone involved has actually agreed to out loud: nothing

70
00:04:13,074 --> 00:04:15,334
ships until the named owner says yes.

71
00:04:15,334 --> 00:04:16,983
That's the whole mechanism.

72
00:04:16,983 --> 00:04:21,259
It works because everyone knows it's real, not because it's elaborate.

73
00:04:21,809 --> 00:04:26,673
Next lecture puts everything from this section together into one document: the

74
00:04:26,673 --> 00:04:31,725
actual checklist, with every question, every owner, and every checkpoint from the

75
00:04:31,725 --> 00:04:35,904
last five lectures in one place you can run on your own next batch.

76
00:04:35,904 --> 00:04:40,893
Before that, write down, right now, who actually owns each of the four checks on

77
00:04:40,893 --> 00:04:45,820
your own account, even if the honest answer to all four is still your own name.

78
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:50,809
That's the first line of B05, the checklist next lecture assembles, and the gate

79
00:04:50,809 --> 00:04:52,992
only holds once the owner is named.
